{
  "statement": "The push for AI-driven MVP velocity often creates an illusion of speed, widening the gap between executive ambition and engineering reality. Executives, seeking rapid market validation, view AI as a shortcut to deploy features faster. This perspective frequently underestimates the complex, human-intensive work needed to integrate, test, and document inherently opaque AI-generated code. Consider the early wave of SaaS companies that prioritized rapid feature delivery over architectural integrity; many faced costly, multi-year platform rewrites years later. While initial AI-assisted coding might seem 30% faster, this can lead to a 50-70% increase in long-term maintenance costs and a 20% spike in critical defect rates, directly impacting customer satisfaction and revenue. To mitigate this liability, implement mandatory, joint executive-engineering review gates. These gates must evaluate code quality and long-term maintainability *before* significant downstream resources are committed, adding an estimated 5% overhead to project planning but reducing future rework by 25%.\n\nThis perceived 'velocity' masks a significant 'Deployment-Capability Lag,' where an initial burst of code generation fails to translate into a robust, deployable product. Without rigorous human oversight, AI-generated code often lacks architectural coherence and comprehensive test coverage. Early studies suggest AI-generated test coverage can be 20-30% lower than human-written code. This deficiency creates unexpected integration nightmares and higher defect rates post-launch. A 90-day MVP, built on this fragile foundation, might require an additional 60-90 days just to meet deployment readiness standards. This effectively doubles time-to-market if not managed, potentially costing millions in lost first-mover advantage and market share. To counteract this, mandate a \"human-in-the-loop\" code review process for all AI-generated code. Focus these reviews on test coverage, documentation, and architectural fit. This adds an estimated 10-15% overhead to the initial coding phase but demonstrably reduces post-deployment defects by 40%.\n\nConsequently, many AI-accelerated MVPs fall into the 'Proof-of-Concept Trap,' transforming a quick market test into a terminal maintenance burden. What begins as an AI-driven \"throwaway\" often becomes the core product due to market pressure. However, its fragile, AI-generated foundation makes it prohibitively expensive to evolve, refactor, or even understand. This locks development teams into a costly cycle of patching and firefighting rather than innovation. This technical debt also impacts talent retention, as frustrated engineers may seek more fulfilling work, increasing turnover by 15-20% in affected teams. This diversion of 30-40% of engineering resources from new feature development to maintenance directly hinders future innovation and market responsiveness. For any AI-assisted MVP, establish a clear, pre-defined \"sunset or rewrite\" strategy with measurable thresholds for technical debt. Budget 20-25% of the initial MVP development cost for a potential rewrite or significant refactor within 12 months if the product gains traction. This treats it as an essential investment, not an afterthought.",
